Q: Why isn&#39;t this simple Bootstrap Confirmation working? A: <p>There are a few important dependencies missing for the implementation:</p>
<ol>
<li>You need to include the specific library that provides the <code>$().confirmation()</code> function:</li>
</ol>
<pre class="lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>     &lt;script src=&quot;https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/bootstrap-confirmation2@2.4.4/bootstrap-confirmation.min.js&quot;&gt;&lt;/script&gt;

</code></pre>
<ol start="2">
<li>The setup script to bind the confirmation to the DOM elements:</li>
</ol>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>    &lt;script&gt;
    $('[data-toggle=confirmation]').confirmation();
     &lt;/script&gt;

</code></pre>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;html&gt;
&lt;head&gt;
  &lt;meta charset="utf-8"&gt;
  &lt;title&gt;Bootstrap Confirmation Example&lt;/title&gt;

  &lt;link href="https://netdna.bootstrapcdn.com/bootstrap/3.3.1/css/bootstrap.min.css" rel="stylesheet"&gt;
  &lt;link href="https://bootstrap-confirmation.js.org/assets/css/docs.min.css" rel="stylesheet"&gt;
  &lt;link href="https://bootstrap-confirmation.js.org/assets/css/style.css" rel="stylesheet"&gt;

  &lt;script src="https://code.jquery.com/jquery-1.11.2.min.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
  &lt;script src="https://netdna.bootstrapcdn.com/bootstrap/3.3.1/js/bootstrap.min.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
  &lt;script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/bootstrap-confirmation2@2.4.4/bootstrap-confirmation.min.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
&lt;/head&gt;

&lt;body&gt;

  &lt;div class="bs-example"&gt;
    &lt;a class="btn btn-large btn-primary" data-toggle="confirmation" data-original-title="Open Google?"
       href="https://google.com" target="_blank"&gt;Confirmation&lt;/a&gt;

  &lt;/div&gt;



&lt;script&gt;
$('[data-toggle=confirmation]').confirmation();
 &lt;/script&gt;
&lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>

Q: What idioms exist to make extensible options structs in Rust? A: <p>I think the most commonly used technique is to use an options struct with chainable setter methods:</p>
<pre class="lang-rust prettyprint-override"><code>Options::new().a(5).c(3).build();
</code></pre>
<p>A great example of this sort of thing is <a href="https://doc.rust-lang.org/1.93.0/std/process/struct.Command.html#examples-4" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>process::Command</code></a> (I linked to an example of a complex use of it).</p>
<p>The options struct itself can be implemented something like this:</p>
<pre class="lang-rust prettyprint-override"><code>#[derive(…)] // whatever traits seem appropriate
struct Options {
    a: u32, // private variable
    c: u32
}

impl Options {
  fn new() -&gt; Self { … }
  fn a(&amp;mut self, a: u32) -&gt; &amp;mut Self { self.a = a; self }
  …
}
</code></pre>
<p>Using <code>&amp;mut self</code> like this means that the options have to be used immediately (otherwise you run into lifetime problems), which is why I added a <code>build()</code> to the example above – if you want to be able to store them for future use, you can do so by using <code>self</code> or <code>Box&lt;self&gt;</code> instead (although both have slightly worse performance, except in the case where the options struct is very small and thus <code>self</code> is OK).</p>
<p>Although you can simplify the repetitive code with an external crate or macro, it isn't really worth it.</p>
<p>This technique also has the advantage that you can change the way the options are represented internally without breaking backwards compatibility (by putting logic into the setters), or, e.g., have setting one option disable a different option.</p>
